ut apprenticeships um  actually  the picture hasn't been that fantastic  has it i mean we know that a lot of  businesses uh it is estimated that  something like 5000 big businesses  didn't take up their full entitlement  under the apprenticeship levy and that  400 million  pounds worth of apprenticeship levy  money simply expired because uh you  can't if you don't use it you lose it um  is there anything that you think we need  to do with the apprenticeship  program to make it more user-friendly so  more people can get onto it because  otherwise  uh it's a lot of noise without much  substance isn't it  i absolutely agree and having uh had a  meeting with the ceo of ocado a couple  of years ago when i was the member of  parliament for badly and spent they paid  the levied they can't recruit this is  not right and that's why i'm really  pleased across where shark shall we  support businesses to share that  apprenticeship levy to make sure that  those smaller smes can get the talented  apprentices that that they need in order  to expand but i would also say having uh  visited a really brilliant local company  that's in aerospace produ max  only a couple of weeks ago talking about  apprentices it's really important that  we make sure that those from black asian  and minority ethnic backgrounds also get  that opportunity to get an  apprenticeship with a really fantastic  company that's my approach as the mayor  that's we have some powers over skills  and i was able to launch a six million  pound program to have modular skills  offer for those uh people who've been  made redundant after furlough or young  people have not been able to just get  started after covid so that they can do  shorter programs because there are some  challenges to full-time programs when  people have to pay their bills and earn  a living so it's really an exciting time  for west yorkshire finally we have  devolution i'm six months into the job  but it's a it's a point that now we're  uh able to deliver obviously we want to  work with government to help them  deliver on their leveling up ambitions  and we're here and we're ready to do it  um can i turn to the other big story of  the day as it is pretty much every week  and that is covid um the health  secretary this week came on a bit like  um school headmaster and uh said that if  we didn't do better on mask wearing and  so on then we'd all be back in detention  what's the mood in west yorkshire  well it is a really troubling time  let's not forget that west yorkshire has  been in restrictions for most of the 18  months our businesses and our high  streets have really suffered so many  people have lost so much over these last  uh awful 18 months it's uh it's  troubling looking at the next few months  i was able with my limited powers to be  able to mandate for mask wearing in bus  stations obviously sadiq khan has  different powers he could say across all  of transport musk wearing to be a  priority and to be mandated we will be  working with councils to find the best  way towards christmas but of course  let's not forget it was councils that  really supported us during the last few  months whether that was testing tracking  and tracing  finding uh laptops for kids that didn't  have tech that are rolling out the  vaccination program this is labour  councils particularly in west yorkshire  that have delivered for their community  and i know we'll continue to do so and  to try and keep people safe  tracy brabin um  politics has been consumed in the last  week  if i may  raise this with the aftermath of the  death of david amos and you yourself  obviously um  have been close to this issue  do you what's your take uh if i may ask  for you know 30 seconds  does politics need to be kinder and  gentler  well of course and you'll know um how i  came into parliament that terrible  tragedy when we lost joe cox i said to  my family who were worried when i took  up the role it was a once in a lifetime  lightning strike i